{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 15, :comments ["The reason for <b>therefore He commanded you to make the Sabbath day</b>: He commanded that you do so on the Sabbath day — this is how Rabbi Abraham (Ibn Ezra on Deuteronomy 5:14) explained it. But this is not correct, for we say in the sanctification of the day, 'for this day is the first of all holy convocations, a remembrance of the Exodus from Egypt,' while it is also said of it <b>a remembrance of the work of Creation</b>.\n\nAnd the Rav said in the Guide for the Perplexed (II:31) that the first version (Exodus 20) concerns the honor and glory of the day, for it says (Exodus 20:11), 'therefore YHWH blessed the Sabbath day and hallowed it,' and therefore it gave the reason <b>for in six days YHWH made</b>, whereas here He admonishes us to keep the Sabbath on account of our having been slaves in Egypt, laboring all day against our will with no rest, and He now commands us to cease and rest, so that we may recall the loving deeds of HaShem upon us in bringing us out from slavery to rest. And so in the Sabbath there are included two reasons: to believe in the creation of the world, that there is an Elohim who is Creator, and also to recall the great kindness He did with us, that we are His servants whom He acquired for Himself as servants.\n\nBut this too is not clear to me, for when we rest and do no work on the seventh day, there is in this no remembrance of the Exodus from Egypt, and one who sees us idle from labor has no knowledge of this matter — rather, it is like all the other commandments. But it is a remembrance of the work of Creation, for we rest on the day on which HaShem rested and was refreshed.\n\nAnd it seems more proper to say that since the Exodus from Egypt demonstrates an Elohim who is pre-existent, who renews creation according to His will and is omnipotent, as I explained in the first version of the commandment (ibid. 2), therefore He said here: if a doubt arises in your heart concerning the Sabbath, which demonstrates creation and will and omnipotence, remember what your eyes saw in the Exodus from Egypt, for that is for you a proof and a remembrance. Thus the Sabbath is a remembrance of the Exodus, and the Exodus is a remembrance of the Sabbath — for they will remember it and say: HaShem renews all through signs and wonders and does all according to His will, for He it is who created everything in the work of Creation. And this is the meaning of 'therefore YHWH your Elohim commanded you to make the Sabbath day.'\n\nNow He did not state here the reason for cessation, 'for in six days YHWH made…,' since this has already been mentioned many times in the Torah; rather He said briefly, <b>and the seventh day is a Sabbath to YHWH your Elohim</b> — for He, blessed be He, rested on it and was refreshed — and explained to them that from the Exodus they would know that He spoke and the world came into being and He rested from it.\n\nAnd by way of truth, we may yet add to this: for this version of the commandment is in 'Observe,' expressing fear of the honored and awesome Name, and therefore He commands us to remember the strong hand and the outstretched arm that we saw in the Exodus from Egypt, from which fear comes to us, as it says (Exodus 14:31), 'and Israel saw the great hand which YHWH did upon Egypt, and the people feared YHWH' — 'therefore YHWH your Elohim commanded you to make the Sabbath day,' that the congregation of Israel be the mate of the Sabbath, as is hinted in the words of our Rabbis (Genesis Rabbah 11:9). And the enlightened will understand:"], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 5, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T02:45:49.398894854Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}